        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Marian Grzymisławski work for?\n\nAnswer A: Poznań University of Medical Sciences\n  about: Marian Grzymisławski\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Grzymisławski worked as a faculty member at the Poznań University of Medical Sciences, a prestigious medical school in Poland.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: University of Reading\n  about: Marian Grzymisławski\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 1990-1995\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"In 1990, Grzymisławski was appointed as a visiting professor at the University of Reading, where he worked in the Department of Clinical Medicine.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Here is a potential Wikipedia entry:\n\nMarian Grzymisławski\n\nMarian Grzymisławski was a Polish internist who made significant contributions to the field of internal medicine. Career\n\nGrzymisławski worked as a faculty member at the Poznań University of Medical Sciences, a prestigious medical school in Poland. During his tenure, he taught and conducted research in the department of internal medicine, sharing his expertise with students and colleagues alike. According to a publication by the Polish Academy of Sciences, Grzymisławski was a member of the faculty at the Poznań University of Medical Sciences, where he played an active role in shaping the institution's academic programs and research initiatives. [1]\n\nAdditionally, a biography published by the Polish Medical Association confirms Grzymisławski's affiliation with the Poznań University of Medical Sciences, highlighting his dedication to medical education and research. [2]\n\nReferences:\n\n[1] Polish Academy of Sciences. (2001). Księga pamiątkowa w 50-lecie Akademii Medycznej w Poznaniu. Warsaw: Polish Academy of Sciences. p. 123. [2] Polish Medical Association. (2010). Słownik biograficzny polskich lekarzy. Warsaw: Polish Medical Association. p. 156.\nDocument 2: Marian Grzymisławski\n\nMarian Grzymisławski (born 1955) is a Polish internist who has made significant contributions to the field of internal medicine. Throughout his career, Grzymisławski has been affiliated with several prestigious institutions, including the University of Reading. Early Life and Education\n\nGrzymisławski was born in Warsaw, Poland, on February 12, 1955. He pursued his medical degree at the Medical University of Warsaw, graduating in 1980. Following his graduation, Grzymisławski completed his residency in internal medicine at the University Hospital of Warsaw. Career\n\nGrzymisławski's academic career began in 1985 when he joined the University of Warsaw as a lecturer in the Department of Internal Medicine. During his tenure, he published several papers on cardiovascular disease and hypertension, which garnered international attention. University of Reading (1990-1995)\n\nIn 1990, Grzymisławski was appointed as a visiting professor at the University of Reading, where he worked in the Department of Clinical Medicine. During his five-year tenure, he collaborated with British researchers on several projects, including a study on the effects of diet on cardiovascular health. [1]\n\nOne of Grzymisławski's most notable contributions during this period was his involvement in the Reading Heart Study, a longitudinal study examining the risk factors for cardiovascular disease in the local population. [2] The study's findings, published in the British Medical Journal in 1993, highlighted the importance of early intervention in preventing cardiovascular disease. [3]\n\nGrzymisławski's work at the University of Reading earned him an honorary fellowship from the Royal College of Physicians in 1994. Later Career\n\nAfter leaving the University of Reading in 1995, Grzymisławski returned to Poland, where he continued to work as a consultant internist. He has since published numerous papers on various aspects of internal medicine and has been recognized for his contributions to the field. References\n\n[1] Grzymisławski, M., et al. (1992). \"Dietary patterns and cardiovascular risk factors in a British population.\" European Journal of Clinical Nutrition, 46(2), 131-138. [2] Reading Heart Study Group. (1993). \"Risk factors for cardiovascular disease in a British population: The Reading Heart Study.\" British Medical Journal, 306(6887), 1121-1126.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
